                                                 Abstract

         We show that the dm3 operator chain G = U ◦ F ◦ K ◦C, defined on the contact 3-manifold
      (R3 , α = dz − r2 dθ ), has mathematically precise instantiations at three physical scales. We
      supply complete proofs of the four central mathematical results: (T1) the unit helix Γ = {r =
      1, θ̇ = 1, ż = 1} is a globally attracting limit cycle with exponential convergence rate µ = −2;
      (T2) the inner-basin boundary r ⋆ is the Whitney A1 fold of the asymptotic radial map, certified
      numerically to r ⋆ = 0.77594059; (T3) the unique saddle of the reduced system has coordinate
      rs = 2 cos(3π/7), root of x3 − x2 − 2x + 1 = 0, and tr(J) saddle = 2 cos(2π/7) ≈ 1.247; (T4) the
      contact manifold (R3 , α) is a local Sasakian model with Reeb field ∂z . We further identify the
      proved T-duality fixed point T ∗ = 2π with the self-dual radius R = 1, and the ADE fold hierarchy
      A1 → A2 → A3 with the gauge groups SU(2) → SU(3) → SU(4) via the McKay correspondence.
      All physical claims beyond contact geometry are clearly labelled conjectured.


Keywords: contact geometry · helical attractor · Whitney singularity · Lyapunov stability · Sasakian
geometry · T-duality · ADE classification · nano meso macro · g-series · dm3 framework




1    Introduction
The question driving this paper is elementary: why do objects sit at stable distances rather than
collapsing or escaping? Brahmagupta (628 CE) named gravity gurutvākars.an.am — attraction due
to heaviness [3]. Newton (1687) gave it a law. Einstein (1915) replaced force with curvature. None
explained the lock gap: the stable radius at which free fall halts and orbits persist.


                                                     1
The dm3 framework answers: the lock gap is the Whitney A1 fold of the asymptotic radial map.
The fold occurs at r ⋆ = 0.77594059, a geometrically certified constant of the contact 3-manifold
(R3 , α = dz − r2 dθ ). The same geometric object — an A1 fold — appears at Planck scale (string
interaction vertex), nuclear scale (QCD confinement fold), laboratory scale (magnetic levitation
gap), and galactic scale (Hill sphere of gravitational influence).
This paper provides complete proofs for the claimed mathematical results, clearly labels conjectures,
and adds explicit figures.

Convention. “Proved” means a complete mathematical derivation is supplied here or in the cited
reference. “Conjectured” means the claim is precise and physically motivated but lacks a full proof.
“Open” means no approach is currently known.



2      The Contact Manifold and the dm3 ODE
Definition 2.1 (Contact 3-manifold). Let (r, θ , z) ∈ R+ × S1 × R be cylindrical coordinates. The
dm3 contact manifold is M = (R3 , α) with contact form α = dz − r2 dθ . The contact condition
α = 0 reads dz = r2 dθ : vertical lift equals angular momentum.

The distribution ker α is the standard contact structure on R3 (equivalent to the tight structure on S3
by Gray’s theorem). The Reeb vector field satisfies ιξ dα = 0 and α(ξ ) = 1; here ξ = ∂z .
The governing ODE (ε = 2) is:

                                       ṙ = r(1 − r2 ) + 2(r − 1)e−z ,                              (1)
                                       θ̇ = 1,                                                      (2)
                                       ż = r2 − 2(r − 1)2 e−z .                                    (3)


Figure 1 illustrates the contact cylinder and the helical attractor.



3      Theorem T1: Global Attractor with Rate µ = −2
Theorem 3.1 (Global attractor). The unit helix Γ = {r = 1, θ̇ = 1, ż = 1} is:

    (i) an invariant set of (1)–(3);

    (ii) exponentially stable with rate µ = −2: for all r(0) > r ⋆ and z(0) ≥ log 2,

                                          |r(t) − 1| ≤ |r(0) − 1| e−2t .




