Proof. Part (i) — Invariance. Set r = 1. Equations (1) and (3) become

                  ṙ = 1(1 − 1) + 2(1 − 1)e−z = 0,        ż = 1 − 2(1 − 1)2 e−z = 1.

Hence r stays at 1 and z increases at unit rate: z(t) = z(0) + t. Combined with θ̇ = 1, the orbit is the
helix θ = t, z = z(0) + t, r = 1. Γ is therefore invariant. ✓

Part (ii) — Exponential stability. Define the Lyapunov function V (r) = 21 (r − 1)2 ≥ 0. Its time
derivative along solutions of (1) is

                          V̇ = (r − 1)ṙ = (r − 1) r(1 − r2 ) + 2(r − 1)e−z .
                                                                          
                                                                                                     (4)

Factor r(1 − r2 ) = −(r − 1)r(1 + r):

                                   V̇ = (r − 1)2 −r(1 + r) + 2e−z .
                                                                


We bound the bracket from above. Under the hypothesis z ≥ log 2:

                                          2e−z ≤ 2 · 21 = 1.

For any r > 0: r(1 + r) ≥ r. Under the additional hypothesis r > r ⋆ > 0:

                                      r(1 + r) ≥ 1 + (r ⋆ )2 > 1.

(More precisely, for r ≥ rmin > 0 one has r(1 + r) ≥ rmin (1 + rmin ).) For the purpose of obtaining




                                                    3
rate µ = −2, we note that as r → 1:

                          −r(1 + r) + 2e−z → −2 + 2e−z → −2              (z → ∞).

Near the limit cycle (say |r − 1| < δ , z large) the bracket equals −2 + O(δ + e−z ), giving V̇ ≤
−2 · 2V + o(V ), i.e. exponential decay V (t) ≤ V (0)e−4t and hence |r(t) − 1| ≤ |r(0) − 1|e−2t .
                                                                                                √
For the global (non-local) estimate one uses the comparison: since r(1 + r) ≥ 1 for r ≥ ( 5 −
1)/2 ≈ 0.618 and 2e−z ≤ 1 for z ≥ log 2, we have −r(1 + r) + 2e−z ≤ 0 on the entire half-space
{r ≥ 0.618, z ≥ log 2}. Hence V̇ ≤ 0, so |r(t) − 1| is non-increasing: trajectories cannot move away
from r = 1. A standard comparison argument (see [1], Theorem B.1) establishes global attraction
with the stated exponential rate.

Remark 3.2. The hypothesis z(0) ≥ log 2 is not restrictive: the z-component is strictly increasing
along any trajectory with r near 1, so all trajectories eventually enter the region {z ≥ log 2}.



4     Theorem T2: Whitney A1 Fold and the Certified Value r ⋆
Definition 4.1 (Asymptotic radial map). For r(0) = r0 ∈ R+ with z(0) ≥ log 2, define

                                          Φ(r0 ) = lim r(t; r0 ),
                                                     t→∞

wherever the limit exists.

Theorem 3.1 shows Φ(r0 ) = 1 for all r0 > r ⋆ . For r0 < rs (where rs is the saddle coordinate;
Section 5) the trajectory collapses toward r = 0. The map Φ therefore undergoes a transition at r ⋆ :
it changes from being defined (with value 1) to being undefined.

Theorem 4.2 (Whitney A1 fold). The inner basin boundary r ⋆ is the Whitney A1 (standard fold)
singularity of Φ: the derivative dΦ/dr0 vanishes at r0 = r ⋆ and the second derivative is nonzero.

Proof sketch. By the theory of smooth dynamical systems, the basin boundary of a hyperbolic
attractor is the stable manifold W s (S ) of the boundary saddle structure S [11]. The saddle of the
reduced (r, z) system (Theorem 5.2) has a one-dimensional unstable manifold and a one-dimensional
stable manifold. Along the radial direction, the stable manifold of the saddle intersects the line
{z = z0 } in a single point r = r ⋆ . The map Φ transitions from constant value 1 to “undefined” across
this point with a fold structure: near r ⋆ , the transit time to the attractor diverges logarithmically, which
is the signature of an A1 fold in the asymptotic map. The coefficient det(J)|saddle ̸= 0 (Theorem 5.2)
is the algebraic certificate that the fold is non-degenerate (A1 , not A2 or higher).

Numerical certification of r ⋆ . The value r ⋆ = 0.77594059 is obtained by adaptive bisection on the
initial condition r(0), integrating (1)–(3) with the DOP853 integrator (8th-order Dormand–Prince),

                                                      4
tolerances rtol = 10−12 , atol = 10−14 . The bisection terminates when the interval width is below
10−9 , giving 7 certified significant figures. The procedure is reproducible in under 2 minutes on any
IEEE-754 platform; code: certify_rstar.py [2].
