Remark 5.3 (Minimal polynomial and the value 2 cos(2π/7)). The number 2 cos(2π/7) is a root
of x3 + x2 − 2x − 1 = 0 (the minimal polynomial of 2 cos(2π/7) over Q [10]). The fact that the
Jacobian trace at the saddle of (1)–(3) equals this algebraic number is a non-trivial consequence of
the polynomial identity for the roots of p. Numerically: 2 cos(2π/7) ≈ 1.2470.

The negative determinant det(J)|(rs ,zs ) < 0 certifies that (rs , zs ) is a saddle (one stable, one unstable
eigenvalue direction).

