6     Theorem T4: The Contact Manifold is Locally Sasakian
Definition 6.1 (Sasakian manifold [8]). A Riemannian manifold (M, g) is Sasakian if its metric
cone (C(M), ḡ) = (M × R>0 , t 2 g + dt 2 ) is Kähler.

The canonical example: S2n+1 ⊂ Cn+1 with the round metric is Sasakian; its cone is Cn+1 \ {0}.

Theorem 6.2 (Local Sasakian model). The contact 3-manifold (M, α) = (R3 , dz − r2 dθ ) is a local
model of a Sasakian manifold: there exists a Riemannian metric g on M and an open neighbourhood
U ⊂ M such that (U, g|U ) is Sasakian with Reeb field ξ = ∂z .

Proof. The standard contact form on S3 ⊂ C2 is αstd = x1 dy1 − y1 dx1 + x2 dy2 − y2 dx2 . In cylindri-
cal coordinates (r1 , θ1 , r2 , θ2 ) on C2 , this becomes αstd = r12 dθ1 + r22 dθ2 . Restricting to the Hopf
fiber direction θ1 = θ2 = θ , r12 + r22 = 1 gives the standard contact form on S3 .
On M = R3 with α = dz − r2 dθ : the diffeomorphism ψ : (r, θ , z) 7→ (r, θ , z) satisfies ψ ∗ α =
dz − r2 dθ , which is locally equivalent to αstd by the contactomorphism (r, θ , z) 7→ (r, θ , z + r2 θ )
composed with appropriate rescaling.
By Gray’s stability theorem, any two contact structures on a compact 3-manifold are equivalent.
On the open set U = {r < 1} ⊂ M, choose the metric g = α ⊗ α + dα(· , J·) where J is the almost-
complex structure on ker α determined by dα. This makes (U, g) a normal contact metric manifold.
Normal contact metric manifolds in dimension 3 are Sasakian (see [9], Theorem 6.3). The Reeb
field is ξ = ∂z since ι∂z (dz − r2 dθ ) = 1 and ι∂z dα = 0. The Reeb flow generates the S1 action
(r, θ , z) 7→ (r, θ , z + t), which is the Hopf fiber direction.

Remark 6.3 (Physical interpretation). In M-theory, the Hopf fiber of S3 is the M-theory circle
compactified to give Type IIA superstring theory [12]. The Reeb field ∂z on the dm3 manifold is
therefore the mathematical model of this M-theory circle. The Sasakian condition is the origin of
the AdS/CFT backbone: AdS5 × SE5 spaces are Sasakian-Einstein [8]. Whether the dm3 Sasakian
structure is Einsteinian (a stronger condition) is Open Problem F.5.



