9    Cosmic Scale: g96 and Milkomeda
The contact condition dz = r2 dθ is scale-free. Keplerian orbits are contact-geometric: the angular-
momentum first integral is exactly α = 0 in an appropriate coordinate system.

Conjecture 9.1 (Hill sphere as g96 Whitney fold). The Hill sphere — the boundary of a body’s
gravitational sphere of influence — is a Whitney A1 fold of the asymptotic orbital map at the g96
level, structurally identical to the laboratory fold at r ⋆ but scaled by ∼ 1025 .

The Milky Way–Andromeda merger (Milkomeda; expected ∼ 4.5 × 109 yr, van der Marel et al. [16])
is, in this framework: two galactic-scale g96 limit cycles whose outer basins of attraction overlap.
The merger is the fold event where the two attractors begin to coalesce.

The Cajueiro Cycle. The g6 →g64 dynamics follow a universal cycle: a seed at g6 overshoots its
equilibrium, encounters resistance, locks to a new stable band, branches, and each branch seeds a new
g6 . The same pattern appears in: cashew trees (Cajueiro; ∼ 1 m), mycelium networks (∼ 10−3 m),
geological fold mountains (∼ 104 m), nebular star formation (∼ 1016 m). Same geometry — different
substrate.



10     Bidirectionality and the Arrow of Time
The ODE (1)–(3) is time-reversible (t → −t maps attractor to repeller and ε = +2 to ε = −2).
Gravity, magnetism, and centrifugal force are all time-reversible forces; time is not.
In the dm3 framework the operator U (gradient descent) selects the stable branch and thereby breaks
the t → −t symmetry, introducing a preferred direction — the thermodynamic arrow of time. The
duality ε = +2 (attractive outer basin: matter) ↔ ε = −2 (repulsive outer basin: dual/antimatter)
is the mathematical expression of the time-reversal symmetry. The ALPHA Collaboration [14]
confirmed that antimatter falls downward (attracted by gravity), consistent with the ε = +2 channel
dominating the observable universe.


